      "prompt": "subject_definitions:\n<Picture 1> is the first frame of [Shot 1], an insert on Brigham Young's heavy raised hand against the black of the door jamb.\n<Audio 1> is the take's complete audio track and runs silent for its whole length.\n\nsummary:\n[reference generation + keyframe completion + audio reuse] One 5.88-second take of 1 shot in the open front door of John Ferrier's log villa on a June morning. [Shot 1] begins from <Picture 1> and runs from 00:00 to 00:05. The take carries <Picture 1> through [Shot 1] in that order. <Audio 1> is the complete audio track.\n\nretention_analysis:\n<Picture 1> ([Shot 1] first frame): fully_preserved - subject placement, wardrobe and light.\n<Audio 1>: fully_copy - <Audio 1> is reused 1:1 as the take's complete final audio track.\n\ndetailed_description:\nPhotoreal live-action, Ferrier's farm, Utah, 1860, low side sun, deep black shadow, 35 mm film grain.\n[Shot 1] From 00:00 to 00:05. The shot begins from <Picture 1>: an insert on Brigham Young's heavy raised hand against the black of the door jamb, the fingers spread in a threat, the sunlight from the right hard on the knuckles and the black coat cuff, the white shirt cuff below it; only what <Picture 1> shows is in frame. The camera is at the threshold at the height of a standing man's head, an arm's length from the hand, a 90mm lens. The low sun comes from the right onto the knuckles. At the first frame the raised hand fills the CENTRE of the frame with the fingers spread across the upper third, lit hard from the RIGHT. The camera pushes in on the raised hand across the whole shot, travelling a hand's breadth as the spread fingers close into a fist, from 00:00 to 00:02. At 00:02 the fist drops out of the bottom of the frame, and the drop continues to the last frame of the shot. By 00:05 the camera is pushing in on the raised hand across the whole shot, travelling a hand's breadth through the last frame of the shot, and the action continues with it.\n\noverall_soundscape: The ambience of the open front door of John Ferrier's log villa on a June morning runs under the whole take: its own air, the surfaces underfoot and the fabric of the place around it. Cloth, boots and the small sounds of hands on wood, glass and iron carry the physical action throughout.\n\nnon_diegetic_music: N/A",
